WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.655
Real data often comes with extra rows you do not need:

2
00:00:04.655 --> 00:00:06.056
titles, notes, test entries.

3
00:00:06.056 --> 00:00:10.962
In this lesson, you will learn to remove rows from the top, from the

4
00:00:10.962 --> 00:00:17.620
bottom, and in a repeating pattern, and how to recover when a step removes too much. Let's tidy up!

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.253
Here we have a table with survey start and end times.

6
00:00:23.253 --> 00:00:24.617
Look at Applied Steps:

7
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:27.687
I have already removed some rows from the bottom.

8
00:00:27.687 --> 00:00:31.440
Every row step is recorded, so you can always check it.

9
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.479
Now, Remove Alternate Rows.

10
00:00:50.479 --> 00:00:53.066
It removes rows in a repeating pattern.

11
00:00:53.066 --> 00:00:54.545
You set three numbers:

12
00:00:54.545 --> 00:01:00.460
the first row to remove, how many rows to remove, and how many rows to keep.

13
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.964
Click OK, and Power Query applies the pattern down the whole table.

14
00:01:13.964 --> 00:01:18.100
Notice the step name in Applied Steps: Removed Alternate Rows.

15
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:39.363
Oops!

16
00:01:39.363 --> 00:01:40.815
This table is empty.

17
00:01:40.815 --> 00:01:44.809
The pattern removed far more rows than we wanted. Don't worry.

18
00:01:44.809 --> 00:01:48.440
Just open the step, change the numbers, or delete it.

19
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.586
Next, Remove Bottom Rows.

20
00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:04.550
On the Home tab, open Reduce Rows, then Remove Rows.

21
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:10.100
Type how many rows to remove from the bottom, for example one hundred fifty.

22
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.137
This menu has more helpers:

23
00:02:29.137 --> 00:02:33.840
Remove Top Rows, Remove Duplicates, Remove Blank Rows, and Remove Errors.

24
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.260
Keep them in mind for every cleaning job.

25
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.376
In this second query, I remove rows from the top.

26
00:02:47.376 --> 00:02:52.440
In the formula bar, you can see Power Query writes it as Table dot Skip.

27
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:04.390
Tip:

28
00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:12.200
remove rows by rules, not by hand, so your cleaning works again every time the data refreshes. Nice and clean!

